Exception = Runtime error that stops the normal flow of a program

Types of Exceptions (basic idea)
1.Checked Exceptions
  Checked at compile time
  Example: File not 
Common Checked Exceptions:
-->FileNotFoundException
-->IOException
-->SQLException

2.Unchecked Exceptions
  Happens during runtime
  Example: divide by zero, null value
Common Unchecked Exceptions:
-->ArithmeticException (divide by zero)
-->NullPointerException (using null value)
-->ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException

| Feature      | Checked Exception | Unchecked Exception |
| ------------ | ----------------- | ------------------- |
| When occurs  | Compile time      | Runtime             |
| Must handle? | Yes (compulsory)  | No (optional)       |
| Example      | File not found    | Divide by zero      |

exception Handling:

try → put risky code
catch → handle error
finally → This block always runs, whether exception occurs or not.
throw → Used to manually create an exception
throws → Used in method declaration to declare exceptions

Key Difference: throw vs throws
| Feature | throw                 | throws                    |
| ------- | --------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Usage   | inside method         | in method declaration     |
| Purpose | create exception      | declare exception         |
| Example | throw new Exception() | method() throws Exception |

example : 
public class SimpleExceptionExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        System.out.println("Program started");

        try {
            int a = 10;
            int b = 0;

            int result = a / b; 
            System.out.println("Result: " + result);

        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: Cannot divide by zero");

        } finally {
            System.out.println("This block always executes");
        }

        System.out.println("Program ended");
    }
}

Methods to print exception?

1. e.printStackTrace()

Prints full error details + line number + method call path
Example:
try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
} catch (Exception e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
Output: (example)
java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero
    at Main.main(Main.java:4)

✔ Best for debugging
✔ Shows where error happened

2. e.getMessage()

Prints only the error message

Example:
try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println(e.getMessage());
}
Output :
/ by zero

✔ Clean and simple
✔ No class name, no line number

3. e.toString()

Prints exception name + message

Example:
try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println(e.toString());
}
Output :
java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero

✔ More info than getMessage()
✔ Less detail than printStackTrace()

4. Custom Print (Using System.out.println)

You can print your own message

Example:
try {
    int a = 10 / 0;
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Something went wrong!");
}

✔ User-friendly message
✔ Used in real applications

| Method              | Output                     | Use           |
| ------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------- |
| `printStackTrace()` | Full details + line number | Debugging     |
| `getMessage()`      | Only message               | Simple output |
| `toString()`        | Name + message             | Medium detail |
| Custom print        | Your message               | User display  |

